1
00:00:02,250 --> 00:00:06,710
That is the most beautiful engagement ring ever!

2
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:10,000
You should know, you bought like a billion of them.

3
00:00:11,090 --> 00:00:12,880
You didn't get one.

4
00:00:14,590 --> 00:00:17,930
Tonight's the big night.

5
00:00:14,590 --> 00:00:17,930
How will you ask her?

6
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,100
It'll be perfect. We'll go to her favorite restaurant.

7
00:00:21,270 --> 00:00:24,380
I'll get her a bottle of the champagne she really loves,

8
00:00:24,380 --> 00:00:26,440
therefore knows how expensive it is.

9
00:00:28,150 --> 00:00:33,240
Then, when the glasses are full, instead of proposing a toast, I'll propose.

10
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:35,900
Aw.

11
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:35,900
Sounds perfect!

12
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:39,030
You'll mess up. Let me do it.

13
00:00:39,200 --> 00:00:40,780
I won't mess up.

14
00:00:40,950 --> 00:00:43,160
If she says no, can I have the ring?

15
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:46,710
She won't say no.

16
00:00:43,750 --> 00:00:46,710
If?

17
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,670
Hey.

18
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:51,670
Hey.

19
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,460
Hey, Monica.

20
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,300
Give it.

21
00:00:56,380 --> 00:00:58,300
It's gone.

22
00:00:59,720 --> 00:01:01,430
Phoebe.

23
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,730
Hey.

24
00:01:04,480 --> 00:01:06,730
Hey, Monica!

25
00:01:08,730 --> 00:01:10,770
We're practically kissing.

26
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:51,730
Hey.

27
00:01:50,610 --> 00:01:51,730
Hey.

28
00:01:51,900 --> 00:01:56,780
Anyone free tonight? My boss is hosting a charity event for kids.

29
00:01:56,950 --> 00:01:59,360
And the more people I bring, the better I look.

30
00:01:59,530 --> 00:02:01,070
So Monica, Chandler?

31
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:04,330
We can't go. We're going to dinner. Remember?

32
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:08,160
Oh, my God. I'm so sorry.

33
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:08,160
What's the big deal?

34
00:02:08,330 --> 00:02:13,040
I just get mad when Rachel doesn't remember where we're going.

35
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:15,880
Where are you going?

36
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:20,260
How about you guys?

37
00:02:18,220 --> 00:02:20,260
Open bar?

38
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,850
I think so.

39
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:23,850
I can do that for the kids.

40
00:02:25,060 --> 00:02:29,350
I'll come. I'm making money now. It's time I give some back.

41
00:02:29,520 --> 00:02:32,100
You could give back the money you owe me.

42
00:02:33,020 --> 00:02:35,400
Okay. Have a benefit.

43
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,360
Ross, can you come to a charity event tonight?

44
00:02:41,530 --> 00:02:43,490
Oh. No, I have plans with Elizabeth.

45
00:02:43,660 --> 00:02:46,370
Oh. You're already doing your part for the kids.

46
00:02:48,500 --> 00:02:53,170
It's our last night together before she leaves for camp. To be a counselor.

47
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:59,550
I have a question. All jokes aside. Where is this relationship going?

48
00:02:59,720 --> 00:03:02,840
Wait a minute. All jokes aside? I didn't agree to that.

49
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:07,850
Do you see this as long-term thing?

50
00:03:04,470 --> 00:03:07,850
I don't know.

51
00:03:08,020 --> 00:03:09,890
You are 12 years older than her.

52
00:03:10,060 --> 00:03:12,390
Wait a minute. Does everyone feel this way?

53
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:15,020
Yeah.

54
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:15,020
Yeah, sort of.

55
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:19,900
Uh, wow. Uh, I thought you were making jokes. I had no idea.

56
00:03:20,070 --> 00:03:24,360
You guys are wrong. Yes, there is a chronological age difference.

57
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:28,490
But I never notice it, because she is very mature.

58
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:33,460
It doesn't matter what you guys think. I'm dating Elizabeth, not you.

59
00:03:33,630 --> 00:03:36,080
Not what she said last night.

60
00:03:38,250 --> 00:03:39,960
See, now he could date her.

61
00:03:44,720 --> 00:03:49,100
Will you marry me?

62
00:03:49,350 --> 00:03:51,230
Hey, you marry me.

63
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:01,570
What's going on, little elves?

64
00:04:01,740 --> 00:04:03,950
It's the big night. We wanted to wish you luck.

65
00:04:04,110 --> 00:04:06,280
Yeah. Have the ring?

66
00:04:04,110 --> 00:04:06,280
In my pocket.

67
00:04:06,450 --> 00:04:07,910
Oh.

68
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:13,080
Pheebs.

69
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:13,080
Oh. Ha, ha.

70
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:19,500
Will you guys get out of here? I want this to be a surprise, and she's gonna know.

71
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:22,300
Yeah, guys. Get out of here.

72
00:04:23,800 --> 00:04:26,300
Hey. Hi, guys.

73
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:32,020
You look beautiful.

74
00:04:28,140 --> 00:04:32,020
Aww. Thank you.

75
00:04:37,400 --> 00:04:39,400
What's going on?

76
00:04:39,570 --> 00:04:41,400
We're just really...

77
00:04:41,570 --> 00:04:44,610
excited about this charity event that we have to go to.

78
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:52,080
Here.

79
00:04:50,330 --> 00:04:52,080
Thank you.

80
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:54,450
So what's going on here?

81
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:56,670
Well, uh, this is a silent auction.

82
00:04:56,830 --> 00:05:00,250
They lay the stuff here. You write down your offer.

83
00:05:00,420 --> 00:05:02,050
The highest bid gets it.

84
00:05:02,210 --> 00:05:06,630
No. I know what a silent auction is. What's going on with your hair?

85
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:11,640
Uh, why?

86
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:11,640
No, it's nice.

87
00:05:13,220 --> 00:05:16,100
Nice to see you. So glad you brought someone.

88
00:05:16,310 --> 00:05:18,150
Someone? I brought people.

89
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:20,940
This is Phoebe. Phoebe, this is Mr. Thompson.

90
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:23,230
He's the head of my department.

91
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:23,230
Oh, hi.

92
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,150
And I also brought my friend Joey.

93
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:27,150
Ohh! Shrimp toast!

94
00:05:28,450 --> 00:05:31,530
You know, I don't know where he is.

95
00:05:32,450 --> 00:05:33,910
I hope you're gonna bid.

96
00:05:34,080 --> 00:05:37,660
Actually, I was about to bid on this lovely trip to Paris.

97
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:39,250
Nice choice.

98
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:39,250
Yeah.

99
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:41,380
Good luck.

100
00:05:39,420 --> 00:05:41,380
Thank you.

101
00:05:41,540 --> 00:05:42,710
Ha.

102
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:46,590
Okay, $20.00.

103
00:05:54,270 --> 00:05:57,020
Shut the door! Shut the door!

104
00:05:57,190 --> 00:05:58,520
What's going on?

105
00:05:58,690 --> 00:06:01,020
Guys across the hall are throwing water balloons.

106
00:06:01,190 --> 00:06:05,030
Call the police. That's what I did to kids in my building.

107
00:06:05,190 --> 00:06:07,940
No, it's a water balloon fight. We started it.

108
00:06:08,110 --> 00:06:10,240
Oh. Hmm.

109
00:06:10,410 --> 00:06:14,620
Um, I just stopped by to see if you wanted to see this play tonight.

110
00:06:14,790 --> 00:06:17,000
Um, it's excellent. The director is...

111
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:20,080
Who drank all the kamikazes?

112
00:06:17,160 --> 00:06:20,080
Nobody. We put them in here!

113
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:25,250
You want some?

114
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:25,250
No, I... Okay, okay.

115
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:30,010
Could I talk to you for a second?

116
00:06:25,420 --> 00:06:30,010
Yeah, sure.

117
00:06:30,180 --> 00:06:32,140
Um, so this play, um, what do you think?

118
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:35,390
It's gotten great reviews.

119
00:06:32,300 --> 00:06:35,390
Attack!

120
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,100
Put your balloons down!

121
00:06:37,600 --> 00:06:41,100
You put your balloons down!

122
00:06:41,270 --> 00:06:45,860
Everybody put their balloons down!

123
00:06:47,740 --> 00:06:50,280
Now, this is a nice suit!

124
00:06:53,660 --> 00:06:57,120
Our next item is the romantic trip to Paris.

125
00:06:57,290 --> 00:07:02,120
It goes to Emil Alexander with a high bid of $2300.

126
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:04,420
Oh! So close!

127
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:08,340
Hey, you guys, look. Got me some drinks.

128
00:07:10,050 --> 00:07:13,050
What are you doing?

129
00:07:10,050 --> 00:07:13,050
Open bar.

130
00:07:14,050 --> 00:07:16,300
Finally, our biggest item of the night.

131
00:07:16,470 --> 00:07:19,980
The 22-foot Gentleman's Daysailer sailboat.

132
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:24,980
The winning bid was a whopping $20,000.

133
00:07:25,150 --> 00:07:26,570
I won!

134
00:07:28,110 --> 00:07:30,110
That was my guess!

135
00:07:31,030 --> 00:07:32,320
What? What? What?

136
00:07:32,490 --> 00:07:34,450
I guessed $20,000.

137
00:07:35,030 --> 00:07:39,910
Joey, it is an auction. You don't guess. You buy!

138
00:07:40,080 --> 00:07:41,370
What?

139
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:45,000
I don't have $20,000.

140
00:07:45,170 --> 00:07:48,000
Congratulations on your new boat, Joey Tribbiani.

141
00:07:52,470 --> 00:07:53,930
Joey, sit down.

142
00:07:54,470 --> 00:07:57,720
Forget her. You enjoy this. Whoo!

143
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:16,320
What are you doing?

144
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:18,490
"One nation...

145
00:08:19,490 --> 00:08:24,330
under God, indivisible, with liberty and justice for all."

146
00:08:24,500 --> 00:08:27,670
Ha, ha. I remembered it. The champagne is here.

147
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:30,000
Are you okay?

148
00:08:28,340 --> 00:08:30,000
Yes. Yes.

149
00:08:30,170 --> 00:08:31,670
I'm good. Are you good?

150
00:08:31,840 --> 00:08:33,880
Are you perfect?

151
00:08:34,510 --> 00:08:35,720
Yeah. I'm okay.

152
00:08:35,890 --> 00:08:37,890
I'm a little cold. Can I have your jacket?

153
00:08:38,050 --> 00:08:39,470
Oh. Yeah.

154
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:42,470
Uh, no, you can't have my jacket.

155
00:08:43,350 --> 00:08:45,600
Because then I would be cold.

156
00:08:46,190 --> 00:08:49,980
If you thought you'd be cold, you should have brought a jacket.

157
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:53,400
Uh, other than that, are you okay?

158
00:08:55,610 --> 00:08:57,200
Are you sure you're okay?

159
00:08:57,370 --> 00:08:59,990
Yes. I'm fine.

160
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:05,330
In fact, I've been fine for a long time now and I think...

161
00:09:05,540 --> 00:09:07,120
the reason is you.

162
00:09:08,330 --> 00:09:09,630
That's sweet.

163
00:09:09,840 --> 00:09:11,880
Okay. Um.

164
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:18,300
Before I met you, I had really little life.

165
00:09:18,470 --> 00:09:21,050
And I couldn't imagine growing old...

166
00:09:21,220 --> 00:09:22,430
Oh, my God.

167
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:25,230
I know. But let me say it.

168
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:25,230
Oh, my God. Richard.

169
00:09:25,390 --> 00:09:27,730
What? I'm Chandler.

170
00:09:30,060 --> 00:09:31,610
Oh, that's Richard.

171
00:09:33,570 --> 00:09:35,780
Maybe he won't see us. Richard!

172
00:09:39,910 --> 00:09:43,370
Monica. Chandler.

173
00:09:39,910 --> 00:09:43,370
Hey.

174
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:46,500
I don't know why I did that.

175
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:51,380
It's good to see you.

176
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:51,380
You too. You let your hair grow.

177
00:09:51,540 --> 00:09:54,170
Yeah. Oh, that's right, you always wanted me to.

178
00:09:54,340 --> 00:09:56,130
I see your mustache is back.

179
00:09:56,300 --> 00:09:58,680
Well, my nose got lonely.

180
00:09:58,840 --> 00:10:01,850
And you don't have a mustache, which is good. Ha, ha.

181
00:10:03,810 --> 00:10:07,020
I'm Chandler. I make jokes when I'm uncomfortable.

182
00:10:07,770 --> 00:10:12,690
I'm sorry. Lisa, Monica, Chandler. We used to date.

183
00:10:12,860 --> 00:10:15,610
Richard! No one's supposed to know about us!

184
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,320
See, I did it again.

185
00:10:21,530 --> 00:10:24,080
Chandler, sit down.

186
00:10:21,530 --> 00:10:24,080
I'll sit down.

187
00:10:24,240 --> 00:10:25,290
Good to see you.

188
00:10:25,490 --> 00:10:27,040
Your table is ready, sir.

189
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:29,080
Oh. Good to see you guys.

190
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:29,080
Yes.

191
00:10:29,250 --> 00:10:31,290
If you prefer, this table's available.

192
00:10:32,670 --> 00:10:34,540
That might be fun.

193
00:10:39,220 --> 00:10:40,930
What were you thinking?

194
00:10:41,140 --> 00:10:43,010
I didn't know it was an auction.

195
00:10:43,180 --> 00:10:44,640
I figured I'd take a guess.

196
00:10:44,890 --> 00:10:48,180
Help a charity. Free boat!

197
00:10:48,350 --> 00:10:51,020
Why would a charity give away a free boat?

198
00:10:51,190 --> 00:10:53,980
I don't know. Charity?

199
00:10:51,190 --> 00:10:53,980
Oh!

200
00:10:55,110 --> 00:10:57,530
Well, just buy the damn boat.

201
00:10:58,490 --> 00:11:01,360
Phoebe, don't you think you've had enough to drink?

202
00:11:01,530 --> 00:11:03,660
I'm just helping the kids.

203
00:11:04,990 --> 00:11:07,660
How is you drinking helping the kids?

204
00:11:07,830 --> 00:11:11,830
Because the more I drink, the less there is for the kids to drink.

205
00:11:15,840 --> 00:11:19,170
Mr. Tribbiani. Your contribution brings us...

206
00:11:19,340 --> 00:11:21,840
a big step closer to building the youth center.

207
00:11:22,300 --> 00:11:25,100
Just out of curiosity, how much is that boat worth?

208
00:11:25,260 --> 00:11:27,140
I think it was valued at 19,000.

209
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:29,770
Hey, I was pretty close.

210
00:11:33,020 --> 00:11:35,190
Uh, so, bad news.

211
00:11:35,360 --> 00:11:39,530
Um, I can't buy the boat. I don't have any money.

212
00:11:42,950 --> 00:11:45,870
Joey! Joey, good one!

213
00:11:47,830 --> 00:11:49,160
Good! Very good!

214
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:58,040
So, uh, I think I'm gonna take off now.

215
00:11:53,920 --> 00:11:58,040
You can't leave, Joey!

216
00:11:58,210 --> 00:12:01,380
You agreed to buy that boat. It is a contract.

217
00:12:01,550 --> 00:12:04,220
Plus, if you leave, my boss is gonna kill me.

218
00:12:04,390 --> 00:12:07,350
Well, what am I gonna do, Rach? I don't have that kind of money.

219
00:12:07,510 --> 00:12:09,260
I know. Okay, okay, okay, okay.

220
00:12:09,430 --> 00:12:10,680
This is what we'll do.

221
00:12:10,850 --> 00:12:14,640
We'll go to the next highest bidder, and we'll let them buy it.

222
00:12:14,810 --> 00:12:17,020
Then you're just gonna pay the difference.

223
00:12:17,190 --> 00:12:18,230
Okay.

224
00:12:18,400 --> 00:12:21,440
I don't know why the kids need a youth center anyway.

225
00:12:21,610 --> 00:12:26,280
They should just watch TV after school like I did. And I turned out fine.

226
00:12:27,320 --> 00:12:28,410
Not great.

227
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:33,120
So we're hiding in the bathroom...

228
00:12:33,290 --> 00:12:36,790
Then I sneak out. But before Monica can, her parents come in.

229
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:39,000
I hid in the shower, next thing you know...

230
00:12:39,170 --> 00:12:41,210
they're going at it on the floor.

231
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:42,670
Oh, my God!

232
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:44,170
I got a good one.

233
00:12:44,340 --> 00:12:48,640
I once walked in on both my parents making love to the same guy.

234
00:12:54,810 --> 00:12:59,190
It's so great seeing you guys again. I'd like to make a toast.

235
00:12:59,360 --> 00:13:03,320
Uh, as a poet once said, "In the sweetness of friendship...

236
00:13:03,610 --> 00:13:06,610
let there be laughter and sharing of pleasures...

237
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:08,990
for in the dew of little things...

238
00:13:09,160 --> 00:13:12,120
the heart finds its morning and is refreshed."

239
00:13:12,290 --> 00:13:13,450
Aw.

240
00:13:14,960 --> 00:13:16,210
What?

241
00:13:24,130 --> 00:13:25,760
Oh, my God! You guys!

242
00:13:25,930 --> 00:13:30,010
Before you say anything, have we got a story for you. Guess who we bumped into?

243
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:32,180
Who?

244
00:13:30,180 --> 00:13:32,180
Richard!

245
00:13:32,720 --> 00:13:35,230
Oh. Oh.

246
00:13:36,060 --> 00:13:38,060
I thought you were going out with Elizabeth.

247
00:13:38,230 --> 00:13:42,020
I was. Uh, she was a little busy with a water balloon fight.

248
00:13:42,190 --> 00:13:45,530
Sometimes grownups have commitments they can't get out of.

249
00:13:46,700 --> 00:13:48,910
Maybe she is too young for me.

250
00:13:49,070 --> 00:13:53,370
When I was over there, I felt like I was a babysitter.

251
00:13:53,580 --> 00:13:57,910
I finally see what you were talking about. I don't know what to do.

252
00:13:58,080 --> 00:14:01,500
Just weigh the good stuff about the relationship against the bad.

253
00:14:01,670 --> 00:14:05,880
That's what I did when I first started weighing stuff.

254
00:14:06,970 --> 00:14:11,930
Okay, um, bad stuff. I'm 12 years older than she is.

255
00:14:12,100 --> 00:14:13,890
If school finds out, you're fired.

256
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:17,060
She's leaving for three months.

257
00:14:14,060 --> 00:14:17,060
For camp.

258
00:14:18,600 --> 00:14:20,560
Okay, good stuff. Um...

259
00:14:20,730 --> 00:14:23,690
Well, she's sweet and pretty and...

260
00:14:24,190 --> 00:14:27,940
The only question you need to ask is, do you see a future?

261
00:14:28,110 --> 00:14:31,200
Do you see yourself marrying her?

262
00:14:31,780 --> 00:14:34,950
My God. You did it already. You married her, didn't you?

263
00:14:36,750 --> 00:14:40,330
No, I didn't do that. It's just...

264
00:14:41,210 --> 00:14:45,840
Honestly, no, I don't see a big future with her.

265
00:14:46,800 --> 00:14:49,220
Okay, I think that's your answer.

266
00:14:49,430 --> 00:14:52,390
I gotta talk to her. I hate this part.

267
00:14:52,550 --> 00:14:55,010
Forget Elizabeth. If you're not careful...

268
00:14:55,260 --> 00:14:58,560
you may not get married at all this year.

269
00:15:02,560 --> 00:15:03,730
Rach, Rach.

270
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:06,440
Next highest bidder is at table one.

271
00:15:03,900 --> 00:15:06,440
Great.

272
00:15:06,690 --> 00:15:09,860
The guy who got the Paris trip is at table four.

273
00:15:10,030 --> 00:15:12,360
You care about the guy who won the Paris trip?

274
00:15:12,530 --> 00:15:13,700
It's a trip for two.

275
00:15:14,580 --> 00:15:15,990
Excuse me.

276
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:25,420
Excuse me. Is the person who won the Paris trip at this table?

277
00:15:25,590 --> 00:15:28,090
That was me.

278
00:15:25,590 --> 00:15:28,090
Oh. Enchante.

279
00:15:31,970 --> 00:15:34,720
Uh, is there a Mr. Bowmont at this table?

280
00:15:34,890 --> 00:15:36,720
That's me.

281
00:15:34,890 --> 00:15:36,720
Ah.

282
00:15:36,890 --> 00:15:40,980
Hello. This is your lucky day, Mr. Bowmont.

283
00:15:41,140 --> 00:15:45,190
The, uh, Gentleman Daysailer has become available again.

284
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:48,440
I believe you made a bid of $18,000?

285
00:15:48,610 --> 00:15:51,650
You have to pay that. It's not just a guess.

286
00:15:54,990 --> 00:15:57,530
I was actually relieved, uh, I didn't win the boat.

287
00:15:57,740 --> 00:15:59,490
My wife would've killed me.

288
00:15:59,660 --> 00:16:02,000
Are you kidding? She'll love this boat!

289
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:04,620
What is your wife's name?

290
00:16:02,160 --> 00:16:04,620
It's Pam.

291
00:16:04,790 --> 00:16:08,960
It's Pam. Just imagine this. The Pam.

292
00:16:09,130 --> 00:16:10,880
Aw.

293
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:13,840
I don't think she'd like that.

294
00:16:14,550 --> 00:16:17,600
Okay. Uh, imagine this: The Mr. Bowmont.

295
00:16:17,760 --> 00:16:18,850
Ooh.

296
00:16:19,350 --> 00:16:20,560
I don't think so.

297
00:16:20,720 --> 00:16:23,770
Okay, look. Let me paint you a little picture. All right.

298
00:16:23,940 --> 00:16:28,440
You are setting sail up the Hudson. You've got the wind in your ha... Arms.

299
00:16:30,530 --> 00:16:34,530
You get all that peace and quiet you've always wanted.

300
00:16:34,700 --> 00:16:37,700
You get back to nature. You can go fishing.

301
00:16:37,870 --> 00:16:41,580
You get one of those hats, people call you captain.

302
00:16:41,750 --> 00:16:44,410
And then when you're old, Cappy.

303
00:16:45,870 --> 00:16:48,540
What the hell. It's for a good cause. All right!

304
00:16:48,710 --> 00:16:51,550
No way! It's mine!

305
00:16:48,710 --> 00:16:51,550
What?

306
00:16:53,840 --> 00:16:55,630
The stuff you said, I want that!

307
00:16:55,800 --> 00:16:58,550
But, Joey, you don't have $20,000!

308
00:16:58,720 --> 00:17:02,390
Who cares? I'll make payments! I want the Mr. Bowmont!

309
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:09,940
Oh, my God! You're back!

310
00:17:10,110 --> 00:17:11,820
Let me see your hand!

311
00:17:13,320 --> 00:17:15,070
Why do you want to see my hand?

312
00:17:15,240 --> 00:17:18,030
I want to see what's in your hand, the trash.

313
00:17:21,040 --> 00:17:25,040
Ew, it's all dirty. You should throw this out.

314
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:27,830
Okay.

315
00:17:30,090 --> 00:17:32,590
What did you just do?

316
00:17:30,090 --> 00:17:32,590
What happened?

317
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:36,260
Richard was there. I couldn't do it.

318
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:36,260
Oh, no.

319
00:17:36,430 --> 00:17:40,180
I was gonna do it tomorrow and surprise her. Now you've ruined it.

320
00:17:40,350 --> 00:17:41,800
We didn't.

321
00:17:41,970 --> 00:17:44,720
Who walks into a room and asks to see a person's hands?

322
00:17:45,850 --> 00:17:50,270
A palm reader, a manicurist, a hand doctor.

323
00:17:50,440 --> 00:17:53,520
Glove salesman.

324
00:17:50,440 --> 00:17:53,520
Good one!

325
00:17:55,530 --> 00:17:57,900
This is terrible. What am I gonna do?

326
00:17:58,070 --> 00:18:02,490
She only suspects something. So just throw her off the track.

327
00:18:02,660 --> 00:18:06,120
I'll make her think, uh, marriage is the last thing on my mind.

328
00:18:06,290 --> 00:18:09,000
Yeah. Convince her you're scared of commitment.

329
00:18:09,170 --> 00:18:11,880
I can do that. I've had 30 years of practice.

330
00:18:12,040 --> 00:18:14,090
Being you is finally gonna pay off!

331
00:18:16,720 --> 00:18:18,130
Had to go to the basement...

332
00:18:18,380 --> 00:18:21,970
some idiot keeps stuffing the chute with pizza boxes.

333
00:18:22,550 --> 00:18:24,890
That guy's still doing that?

334
00:18:26,850 --> 00:18:29,390
Hey. My God, let me see your hand!

335
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:33,230
No, you're too late! She already took out the trash!

336
00:18:41,320 --> 00:18:47,120
Wow. I have never had such a healthy breakup.

337
00:18:47,370 --> 00:18:50,620
She was such a grownup about it.

338
00:18:50,960 --> 00:18:54,130
She didn't seem too immature for me.

339
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:58,340
Did I just make a huge mistake?

340
00:18:58,510 --> 00:19:00,090
Ross? Wait!

341
00:19:00,260 --> 00:19:03,720
Elizabeth, thank God. I was just thinking about...

342
00:19:03,890 --> 00:19:05,930
You suck!

343
00:19:06,100 --> 00:19:07,180
What?

344
00:19:13,230 --> 00:19:14,810
Okay.

345
00:19:14,980 --> 00:19:17,780
Breakup's still on.

346
00:19:25,740 --> 00:19:26,870
Here she comes.

347
00:19:27,040 --> 00:19:29,660
Do I look like a guy who doesn't want to get married?

348
00:19:29,870 --> 00:19:31,330
Yeah.

349
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:34,130
And, also, a little like a French guy.

350
00:19:37,250 --> 00:19:39,130
I never noticed that before.

351
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:42,380
Hey, guys.

352
00:19:40,720 --> 00:19:42,380
Hey.

353
00:19:42,550 --> 00:19:43,680
What are you up to?

354
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:46,930
Just hanging out. Talking about, uh, web sites.

355
00:19:47,100 --> 00:19:48,140
Ha.

356
00:19:48,810 --> 00:19:52,440
Ahem. We saw this interesting website about marriage.

357
00:19:52,690 --> 00:19:54,770
How unnecessary it is.

358
00:19:54,980 --> 00:19:57,770
How it's a way for governments to keep tabs on you.

359
00:19:57,980 --> 00:19:59,730
Yeah, Big Brother. Ha, ha.

360
00:20:01,070 --> 00:20:02,400
That's a little crazy.

361
00:20:02,570 --> 00:20:07,030
Although I am glad you're looking at other things on the Internet.

362
00:20:07,910 --> 00:20:11,700
Got me thinking, why would anybody want to get married?

363
00:20:11,870 --> 00:20:16,130
Why? To celebrate the relationship. To solidify the commitment.

364
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:19,130
To declare your love for one another to the world.

365
00:20:20,050 --> 00:20:21,340
Eh.

366
00:20:23,050 --> 00:20:25,090
Okay. Well, that's good to know.

367
00:20:28,100 --> 00:20:29,970
The Mr. Bowmont's here!

368
00:20:36,100 --> 00:20:39,520
A customer wants to compliment you. Shall I let him in?

369
00:20:39,690 --> 00:20:42,070
Sure! I love this part!

370
00:20:42,740 --> 00:20:45,150
Come on in.

371
00:20:42,740 --> 00:20:45,150
Hi.

372
00:20:45,910 --> 00:20:47,160
Richard.

373
00:20:47,370 --> 00:20:49,740
I'm not here to compliment the chef.

374
00:20:49,910 --> 00:20:53,450
Oh. I hate when people come back to compliment the chef.

375
00:20:53,620 --> 00:20:56,040
Like I've nothing better to do.

376
00:20:57,420 --> 00:20:58,500
What's up?

377
00:20:58,670 --> 00:21:01,380
It was great seeing you the other night.

378
00:21:01,550 --> 00:21:03,460
Good to see you too.

379
00:21:05,300 --> 00:21:06,760
Come down here to tell me that?

380
00:21:06,930 --> 00:21:10,430
No. Came here to tell you something else.

381
00:21:12,100 --> 00:21:15,480
Came here to tell you I still love you.

